Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),6−10 simplified as 3- periodic porous crystalline frameworks constructed from metal-containing nodes and organic linkers, represent a promising class of programmable catalytic materials.11−14 $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{W e}}^{\mathsf{\Pi}_{15-18}}$ and others19−24 have explored MOFs as promising candidates for the catalytic hydrolysis of nerve agents and their simulants. Particularly, hexanuclear zirconium cluster-based $\mathbf{MOFs^{25-27}}$ ( $\mathrm{^{\circ}z r}$ -MOFs) with diverse pore geometry, pore sizes, and catalytically active $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ nodes have been explored as catalysts for hydrolysis of nerve agents and their simulants in aqueous alkaline solutions.15−18,28 The need for liquid water and/or volatile bases can be accommodated in fixed-site decontamination such as a chemical agent stockpile but limits their utility as a field protective material.
Notably, MOFs, including $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs, can also adsorb water with a steep uptake (pore filling or condensation) at room temperature, which occurs in a specific relative humidity (RH) range depending on the pore size/geometry and chemical composition.29−37 These inherent water adsorption properties indicate potential in using $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs for the catalytic, hydrolytic destruction of nerve agents under ambient humidity by using the water present in the pores, circumventing the need for liquid water. In this work, we have integrated MOFs and solid, polymeric bases into a native composite material, which can also be grafted onto textile fibers, for the destruction of organophosphonate-based nerve agents under ambient conditions. This is a significant step forward in the integration of MOFs and bases together into a fiber to access a catalytically active protective material, which is a sought-after solution for the on-demand destruction of these harmful chemicals in practical environments.
The water vapor adsorption isotherms (Figure 2A) of ZrMOFsUiO-66,25 UiO- $\mathsf{\bar{66}}\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ 38 MOF-808,29 and NU$1000^{16}$ were measured at $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ after confirming the phase purity and porosity of these materials (Figures S2, S6, S12, and S14). Among them, MOF-808,29 with a 3,6-connected spn net (spn symbolizes spinel), is constructed from tritopic benzene1,3,5-tricarboxylate (BTC) linkers and 6-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ nodes (Figure 1) and has shown remarkable hydrolysis activity with nerve agent simulants in liquid water in the presence of a volatile organic base $.N.$ -ethylmorpholine.17 From the water adsorption isotherms, the pores of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66,\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ and MOF-808 can be saturated with water at $\mathrm{RH}=40\%$ at $298\ \mathrm{K},$ while NU-1000 reaches its saturation at $\mathrm{RH}=70\%$ at $298{\mathrm{~K}}$ This data indicates potential to do catalytic hydrolysis with the former under ambient humidity $(\sim50\%)$ since the necessary water molecules for the reaction would be present inside the pores of the MOFs. Recently, we have also reported the replacement of volatile and toxic organic bases, traditionally used in hydrolysis, with heterogenized polymeric baseslinear polyethylenimine (PEI)in order to achieve more facile detoxification of nerve agents.39,40 Thus, PEI was targeted as the solid-phase base to regenerate Lewis acidic $\mathrm{z}\mathrm{r}$ sites during the catalysis.
The MOF/PEI composites were synthesized from simple vortex mixing of MOF powders (i.e., UiO-66, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ MOF-808, and NU-1000) and ground PEI particles. These materials displayed impressive catalytic performance under ambient humidity $\mathrm{{'RH}=50\%}$ ) toward the destruction of dimethyl 4-nitrophenyl phosphonate (DMNP), a nerve agent simulant (Figure S1). All MOF/PEI composites with $6\mathrm{mol}\%$ MOF loading showed higher conversions of DMNP without using liquid water compared to the pure MOF or PEI (Table S2). Control experiments under MOF digestion conditions revealed that the contribution from the background reaction was negligible (Figure S15). This demonstrates the efficacy of MOFs for the hydrolysis of phosphate ester bonds under reasonable RH. Among studied composites, MOF-808/PEI prepared from MOF-808 microcrystals with sizes of $2{\mathrm{-}}4\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ showed the highest activity in degrading DMNP at $\mathrm{RH}=50\%$ . This can be attributed to the combined contribution of the following: (i) the large aperture of MOF-808 allows facile diffusion of reactants to the catalytic sites; (ii) the 6-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node offers more active sites for reaction, as opposed to higher connectivities in the other MOFs; and (iii) the pore can be filled with water at $\mathrm{RH}=50\%$ .
Kinetic studies revealed an initial half-life of $t_{1/2}\approx1.6\mathrm{~h~}$ (turnover frequency, $\mathrm{TOF}\approx5.2\:\mathrm{h^{-1}},$ ) for hydrolysis of DMNP in the presence of MOF-808/PEI composite ( $6\mathrm{mol}\%$ catalyst loading) under $\mathrm{RH}=50\%$ (Figure 2B). It is also worth noting that there were MOF particles which were not in contact with DMNP due to the high viscosity of DMNP, which could lead to poor mixing. This, in turn, would lead to a slight underestimation of the catalytic activity. Additionally, we have observed that the RH under which the reaction is carried out can play a significant role in the catalytic activity, as it dictates the amount of water available in the pores. For example, about a $60\%$ enhancement in conversion of DMNP was observed for NU-1000/PEI when RH was increased from $50\%$ to $85\%$ the water adsorption isotherm revealed that condensation happens at $\mathrm{RH}\approx60\%$ and $298{\mathrm{~K}}.$ On the other hand, a slightly enhanced performance was observed for MOF808/PEI composites toward hydrolyzing DMNP under $\mathrm{RH}=$ $70\%$ with $t_{1/2}\approx1.2\mathrm{h}$ and $\mathrm{TOF}\approx\mathrm{\dot{7}.0~\dot{h}^{-1}}$ , indicating that the effect of increasing humidity on the catalytic activity is limited after the pore-filling RH is reached. To the best of our knowledge, this represents the first example of the use of MOF composites for the efficient catalytic hydrolysis of nerve agent simulants without using liquid water.
While MOFs showed promising catalytic activity, their employment as protective layers requires the integration of these fine powders into existing fabrics.41 The resulting composite material needs to be reactive and impermeable to agents. To this end, we employed air-permeable, flexible, and non-toxic textiles as inexpensive and readily accessible supports for MOF/PEI composites. A gel-based dip-coating method42 was used to prepare a MOF/polymer coating on a flexible fiber.
Briefly, MOF-808 nanoparticles with sizes of $100{-}200~\mathrm{nm}$ were dispersed in a PEI solution in methanol, which was used as a stock suspension for coating. After a dipping and drying process at room temperature (see the Supporting Information for more details), a dense and continuous MOF-808/PEI composite coating was obtained on the fiber with a mass loading of $12\%$ (Figures 3, S3, S4, S7−10, and S13). It is to be noted that the current method reported here is highly scalable and inexpensive compared to previous methods of MOF/fiber preparation techniques.43,44 Kinetic studies of DMNP hydrolysis with MOF-808/PEI/fiber under $\mathrm{RH}=50\%$ revealed that the initial half-life is rapid $\left(t_{1/2}\approx0.4\mathrm{h}\right)$ (Figures 4, S16, and S17), which is approaching the activity of some $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs in the presence of volatile organic bases and liquid water.15 Nearly no hydrolysis products were observed when using cotton fiber, MOF-808-coated fiber, and PEI-coated fiber as controls in tests under $\mathrm{RH}=50\%$ for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . The enhanced activity of the MOF-808/PEI/fiber system compared to MOF808/PEI powder can attributed to the highly uniform blend and dispersion of the MOF/PEI on the fiber surface, which could expose more catalytic sites and better exploit wicking effects of agents compared to a physical mixture of powders.
With these promising simulant results, solid-state tests using the MOF-808/PEI/fiber were conducted on the nerve agent soman (GD) (Figures 5, S18, and S19). Notably, the composite was even more reactive toward the nerve agent than the simulant, exhibiting a half-life of only 12 min $(\sim0.\bar{2}\mathrm{h})$ and a rate constant of $5.0\mathrm{{h}^{-1}}$ using a first-order kinetic model. GD was almost completely degraded after only $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to nontoxic pinacolyl methylphosphonic acid (PMPA). Moreover, V X ( O - e t h y l S - [ 2 - ( d i i s o p r o p y l a m i n o ) e t h y l ] - methylphosphonothioate) was completely hydrolyzed after only $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ under the same conditions to the non-toxic products EMPA (ethyl methyl phosphonic acid) and DESH [2- (diisopropylamino)ethanethiol] (Figures S20 and S21). This finding demonstrates the capability of this composite for cleaving both $\mathrm{P-F}$ and $\mathrm{P}{-}S$ bonds of nerve agents under ambient conditions.
Despite the use of an aqueous solution of volatile $N\cdot$ - ethylmorpholine in the recently reported electrospun UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ /fiber composite,45 our MOF-808/PEI/fiber tested without liquid water showed reactivity comparable to the hydrolysis of DMNP $\left(t_{1/2}\ \approx\ 24\right.$ min vs ${\sim}12{-}210\ \mathrm{\min},$ . Additionally, MOF-808/PEI/fiber also exhibited superior activity for hydrolysis of GD $\left(t_{1/2}\right)=\sim12$ min vs ${\sim}131\ \mathrm{min}$ ). Importantly, MOF-808/PEI showed significantly higher activity for DMNP hydrolysis when compared to that when only MOF was used under similar conditions ( ${\sim}0.4$ h vs ${\sim}24$ h).46
High robustness and durability of the MOF-coated fiber are essential in practical applications, as the peeling or degradation of MOF particles could reduce the lifespan of a protective cloth. The adhesion of MOF composite coating to cotton fiber was tested through agitation of the sample in water for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . No notable loss of crystallinity and sample was observed (Figures S5 and S11), and quantitative zirconium analysis by ICP-OES showed that $\mathrm{Zr}$ content was nearly unchanged after agitation in water (Table S1). Additionally, the composite’s catalytic performance remained intact after drying in air at room temperature. We attributed the stability of the MOF808/PEI/fiber to the low solubility of the PEI in water at room temperature, which can act as a binder. The shelf life of the composite was also investigated. Strikingly, after storage in air for 100 days, the catalytic activity for DMNP hydrolysis was within the experimental error (conversion at $30~\mathrm{\min}$ : as prepared, ${\sim}57\%$ vs after 100 days, ${\sim}68\%$ ). We have also demonstrated the scalability of the current method, where a larger piece of textile composite $(30~\mathrm{~cm~}\times~30~\mathrm{~cm})$ was prepared and the resulting composite showed similarly high catalytic activity.
Under relevant conditions, a protective cloth was exposed to numerous other conditions aside from humidity, such as sweat, atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and heavy contaminants (i.e., octane) from vehicles. The performance of the MOF-808/PEI/fiber was evaluated under simulated conditions. After addition of $10~\mu\mathrm{L}$ of artificial perspiration $\left(\mathrm{pH}=4.5\right)$ on a MOF-808/PEI/fiber composite ( $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\omega~}_{\mathrm{1}}\mathrm{cm}\times1\mathrm{cm},$ , the catalytic conversion of DMNP at $0.5\mathrm{h}$ increased from $57\%$ to $94\%$ (Figure 4). After the sweat composites were dried in air, the catalytic activity was comparable to that of fresh sample, implying that the enhanced catalytic activity can be attributed to the water in the sweat. Additionally, MOF-808/PEI/fiber composite retained its catalytic activity after exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and simulated diesel atmosphere.
Herein, we have developed a composite material composed of a MOF-808/PEI/fiber which showed high catalytic activity for the hydrolysis of a nerve agent under relevant conditions. More importantly, despite utilizing only the water inside the pores of the MOF crystals, this composite material showed enhanced activity for the hydrolysis of DMNP and GD compared to many previously reported $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs/volatile organic base systems tested in bulk water. Durability tests illustrated the robustness of these textile materials under reallife atmospheres and conditions, highlighting their potential for incorporation into protective layers against CWAs. We believe the findings here are a crucial step toward the widespread implementation of MOF materials in protective gear applications.
